# C++ structure

Naming → [naming.mdc](naming.mdc). Python: [../python/naming.mdc](../python/naming.mdc). Bash: [../bash/naming.mdc](../bash/naming.mdc).

Conventions for code with state machines and a hardware/control layer. Applies to **new code** — do not refactor legacy unless asked.

## Header (.hpp) vs source (.cpp)

| What | Where |
|------|-------|
| Method declarations, shared constants, small helpers | `.hpp` (or `*_helpers.hpp`) |
| `#include` required by declarations in the header | `.hpp`, not only `.cpp` |
| Data structs used by the class | outside the class, in the same `.hpp` |
| Method implementations | `.cpp` |
| Constants **only for this translation unit** | `static constexpr` in `.cpp` |

Avoid an anonymous namespace in `.cpp` for helpers/constants that may be needed in another file — that hides API.

## Constants

- Format: `k_snake_case` (e.g. `k_mode_confirmation_timeout_ms`).
- Shared → `inline constexpr` or `static constexpr` in `.hpp`.
- Private to one `.cpp` → `static constexpr` at the top of the file.
- Prefer `static constexpr` over `#define`.

## Methods instead of monoliths

Extract **private methods** when the main handling loop has more than one responsibility, or the same condition is repeated — **one gate high**.

```cpp
void ProcessTick(...) {
    const ConnectionState current_state = ...;  // snapshot at the start
    if (!IsReady(current_state)) { ...; return; }
    if (output_enabled) {
        WritePrimaryCommands();
        WriteSecondaryCommands();
    } else {
        ZeroReleasedOutputs();
    }
}
```

Keep getters/setters in one block, with no logic between them. Reuse existing module API before writing your own loop (e.g. `IsErrorActive()` from a shared library).

## Variables — only when they earn their keep

Do not introduce a local used **exactly once** — inline the expression.

```cpp
// Bad — noise
const auto name = "session_" + storage.prefix;
set_flag(name + "/error", true);

// Good
set_flag("session_" + storage.prefix + "/error", true);
```

Exceptions: a **snapshot** used more than once in the same function (`current_state`); a complex expression where the name helps; a copy under a mutex before releasing the lock.

## State machines — `enum class`, not a set of flags

When an object moves through states (lifecycle, connection, activation), model that as one enum — pattern: `ConnectionState` + `ConnectionStateToString()`.

```cpp
enum class ConnectionState
{
    DISCONNECTED,
    CONNECTING,  // comment when the state is not obvious
    ACTIVE,
    FAULT,
};

inline const char* ConnectionStateToString(ConnectionState s)
{
    switch (s) {
    case ConnectionState::DISCONNECTED: return "DISCONNECTED";
    // ...
    }
    return "UNKNOWN";
}

std::atomic<ConnectionState> connection_state_{ConnectionState::DISCONNECTED};
```

- One `enum class` per state axis; one field — do not use a pair of bools if they can be merged.
- Values: `SCREAMING_SNAKE`, short. `FooToString`: `inline const char*` next to the enum in `.hpp`.
- Logic: `switch (stage)` / `if (stage == ConnectionState::X)` instead of `if (is_a && !is_b && is_c)`.

Exception: separate bools only when they describe **independent** facts (`enable_requested_` vs `connection_active_`), not successive steps of the same machine.

## Readability

- Blank line between logical blocks and **before** a comment.
- Technical comments: precise (e.g. “output FET off”, not a vague “power off”).
- TODO: one line + issue number. Example: `// TODO(#123): re-handshake after failed Activate`.
- Logs: do not duplicate — if a helper already logs, do not add a second framework-logger call.

## Shared state across threads

- Snapshot at the start of the main handling loop: `const ConnectionState current_state = connection_state_.load()` under the mutex.
- Shared flags: `std::atomic` or mutex plus a comment why.
- When adding a thread: consider mutex/CV and document ordering assumptions — details in the MR/issue.

## Exclusions

Do not change framework API or virtual overrides without need. Product decisions belong in an issue/MR, not this rule.
